@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+# confkit: in-repo parser replaces inilite
+
+## What changed
+
+- `confkit/parse.py`: the `inilite` wrapper is replaced by a full parser for `docs/FORMAT.md`. It implements `loads(text, base_dir=None, source="<string>")` and `load(path)`. It covers lines and comments, names and dotted keys, sections, entries, multi-line values, quoted strings with escapes, lists, typed scalars, `@include` with cycle detection and per-file sources, and the duplicate and conflict checks. Every error is a `ParseError` with the line number from the spec. Nothing is imported from `vendor/`, and no dependency was added.
+- `tests/test_format.py`: new file with 51 tests for the parts of the spec that `tests/test_parse.py` does not cover. No existing test was edited.
+- No other files were changed. `vendor/` is untouched and still present.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `python -m unittest` (run from the repo root): 64 tests, all OK. That is the 13 existing tests plus the 51 new ones.
+- `python -m confkit` with no arguments: prints the usage line and exits 2. Before the change, this crashed at import.
+- The CLI paths (JSON output, `--get`, missing key exit 2, parse error exit 1) are exercised by subprocess tests in `tests/test_format.py`, and they pass.
+- Spot checks run from the command line, outside the test suite: `[a]` followed by an indented line gives `continuation without a value` at line 2. A bad escape on a later duplicate reports the value error first. `a = 1` followed by `a.b = 2` gives `key conflict`. CRLF and CR line endings work. Entries continue across blank and comment lines.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- The old `inilite` was not compared against, because `vendor/` may only be read for reference and I could not run it. Behaviour is checked only against `docs/FORMAT.md` and the existing tests.
+- No fuzzing or property testing was done.
+- Choices where the spec was silent or ambiguous:
+  - `@include"x"` with no whitespace after the word is `invalid include`.
+  - Quote characters only start a quoted string at the start of a value or a list item. In a plain value, `#` or `;` after whitespace always starts a comment.
+  - In a list, an unquoted item runs to the next comma, bracket, newline or comment, so `[1 2 "x"]` is one string item.
+  - The source of a nested include is `os.path.normpath(os.path.join(...))`. Its directory for resolving further includes is `os.path.dirname` of the joined path before normalising. These differ only when symlinks or `..` are involved.
+  - Messages carry a detail after the required prefix, e.g. `duplicate key 'a.b'`.
+- `load()` on a root file that is not valid UTF-8 raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, which is a `ValueError` but not a `ParseError`. The spec does not cover this case, so I left it alone.
+- `vendor/inilite` is still in the repo. Nothing imports it now, so it can be deleted, but that change falls outside this task's file list.
